Case Report
Status spasticus and psoas muscle edema due to 
anti-GAD antibody associated stiff-man syndrome
Boby Varkey Maramattom
Abstract
Severe muscle rigidity and spasms are uncommon causes of Intensive Care Unit (ICU) 
admissions. Stiff-man syndrome (SMS) is a rare disorder characterized by continuous 
muscle spasms, axial muscle rigidity, “tin soldier gait,” and continuous motor unit activity 
on electromyography. There are three clinical variants of SMS; stiff-limb syndrome, 
classical SMS, and paraneoplastic encephalomyelitis with rigidity and myoclonus. 
Three types of antibodies have been associated with SMS; however, anti-glutamic acid 
decarboxylase (GAD) antibodies are the most frequent and are seen in the idiopathic 
type of SMS. The spasms of SMS can be very disabling and severe enough to cause muscle 
ruptures and skeletal fractures. We present a case of anti-GAD positive SMS with “status 
spasticus” causing bilateral psoas myoedema and rhabdomyolysis due to repeated axial 
muscle jerking in a 64-year-old man and discuss the differential diagnosis of a “jerking 
patient in the ICU.”
Keywords: Psoas myoedema, rhabdomyolysis, status spasticus, stiff-man syndrome
