injury as in status dystonicus. Neuromuscular blockers 
and mechanical ventilation may be required in status 
spasticus to prevent muscle rupture, muscle hematomas, 
rhabdomyolysis, or bone and joint injuries.
[3,4]
The rigidity and “jerks” are treated with GABAergic 
drugs such as benzodiazepines, baclofen or vigabatrin, 
IVIG, and plasmapheresis or rituximab.
[5] In exceptional 
cases, intrathecal baclofen pumps have been implanted.[6] 
In conclusion, our patient had a jerking SMS and “status 
spasticus” associated with anti-GAD antibodies and 
hitherto undescribed MRI features such as bilateral psoas 
muscle edema. The peculiar muscle involvement on MRI 
should be a pointer toward axial muscle predilection and 
guide evaluation and therapy.
